use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Filtering:
    description:
        Filtering narrows a list of options down to the ones that match what
        the user typed, and puts them in order. It sits between a text box and
        the list of results, and belongs to the component that has both.

    query:
        The text the user has typed. If it is empty, every option is shown in
        its original order.

    matching:
        An option matches if the typed text appears anywhere in its label, as
        one unbroken piece. Typing `ran` matches `Orange`. Matching only the
        start of the label, or letters scattered through it, are different
        rules altogether, not settings a component can switch to.

    matchText:
        By default an option is matched on its label. The component can supply
        other text to match on instead, such as the label plus some
        alternative names, so that `USA` finds `United States`.

    normalisation:
        Upper and lower case are treated the same, and accents are ignored, so
        `cafe` matches `Café`. Spaces at the start or end of what was typed are
        ignored; spaces in the middle count.

    ordering:
        Options where the match appears earlier in the label come first. Ties
        keep their original order. Nothing else affects the order.

    groups:
        If the options are in groups, the groups are dropped while the user is
        searching and all matches are shown as one list. The groups come back
        when the search is cleared.

    disabledOptions:
        Disabled options are matched like any other and still appear in the
        results, shown as disabled. Options that are already selected are also
        matched like any other; whether to hide them or keep them at the top
        is up to the component.

    matchPositions:
        Each match says where in the text it was found, so the matching part
        can be highlighted. Knowing only that an option matched, and not
        where, is not enough.

    emptyResult:
        If nothing matches, the result is empty. It does not fall back to
        showing everything. What to show instead, such as "No results", is up
        to the component.

    source:
        All the options are already available when the user types. Fetching
        options from a server as the user types is a separate problem, with
        its own concerns like waiting, cancelling, loading and errors, and is
        not covered here.

    nonGoals:
        - No fuzzy matching, where `apl` would find `Apple`. It only works well
            with a way of ranking results, which is a bigger decision than this
            behaviour should make
        - The first result is not highlighted automatically. Whether pressing
            Enter picks the top result without the user moving to it is up to
            the component
